---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const mlCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Split first: train / test', note: 'Hold out data the model never sees. Train on one part, evaluate on the other. Skipping the split = grading yourself on questions you memorised.' },
    { math: '2 · Baseline before model', note: 'Predict the average, or the majority class. If a fancy model can\'t beat this baseline, the fancy model is the problem.' },
    { math: '3 · Overfitting: memorising the noise', note: 'A model complex enough can fit training data perfectly and fail on new data. Watch the gap between train and test performance — the gap IS the overfitting.' },
    { math: '4 · Fix with regularisation and more data', note: 'Simpler models, fewer features, penalties on complexity, cross-validation, or more examples. The bias–variance trade-off is the field\'s central dial.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/data-science/advanced/machine-learning-basics">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Machine learning is <strong>fitting functions to data and hoping they generalise</strong>.
    Supervised learning (labelled examples → predict labels), unsupervised (find structure
    without labels), reinforcement (learn from reward signals). The craft is managing the
    tension between fitting the training data and generalising beyond it — the bias–variance
    trade-off.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the working loop — split, baseline, fit, diagnose, regularise" widget="equationStep" config={mlCfg} />

  <h2>The model families, honestly compared</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Linear/logistic regression:</strong> transparent, fast, surprisingly hard to beat on tabular data. Always try first.</li>
    <li><strong>Tree ensembles (random forest, XGBoost):</strong> the default winners on tabular business data — handle mixed types, missing values and interactions with little tuning.</li>
    <li><strong>Neural networks:</strong> dominant on images, audio, text — where raw structure matters. Data-hungry and opaque (the AI ladder covers why).</li>
    <li><strong>k-means / clustering:</strong> the unsupervised default for segmentation — but cluster count and evaluation are judgement calls, not outputs.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Evaluation is the real skill</h2>
  <p>
    Accuracy lies on imbalanced data (fraud at 0.1%: "always say no" is 99.9% accurate).
    Precision/recall trade-offs, ROC curves, and confusion matrices are the honest instruments —
    and the metric must match the decision ("missed fraud costs 100× a false alarm" changes the
    threshold entirely).
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Géron, Hands-On Machine Learning; Hastie et al., Elements of Statistical Learning; scikit-learn documentation.</p>
</TopicPage>
